"Retrograde Motion and World Models | VirtualDemos",
"Retrograde Motion and World Models",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Mars loops, {#} to {#}; Mars close up, {#}; Jupiter, {#}; Saturn, {#}; Mercury’s three loops a year; Oppositions of Mars, {#} to {#}; Earth held still (Tycho); Ptolemy’s epicycle for Mars; Copernicus’s circles miss; Phases of Venus; Galileo’s test: Ptolemy’s Venus. This worksheet uses Mars loops, {#} to {#}, Phases of Venus and Galileo’s test.",
"The large picture shows the solar system from above the North Pole, with Earth’s orbit in blue. A yellow line of sight runs from Earth through the planet out to the stars, and the name at its end is the constellation the planet is in. Numbered lines of sight, a few weeks apart, match the numbered points on the sky below.",
"The strip along the bottom is the sky seen from Earth along the ecliptic, the Sun’s yearly path, with real stars and constellations. East is to the left, as when you face south from the Northern Hemisphere. The orange line is the path the planet has taken so far; the dashed line is where it will go. Stationary points, oppositions and other events are labeled on the path. Drag the strip sideways to look along the sky.",
"The small round picture is the planet through a telescope, at its true size (the field is {#} arcseconds across): you can see its phase and how big it looks.",
"The World model switch makes the same sky three ways: Kepler (the real sky, from NASA JPL orbit data), Copernicus (circles around the Sun) and Ptolemy (epicycles around a fixed Earth). Hold still: Earth shows the same orbits with Earth held still, which is Tycho Brahe’s system.",
"Play runs time forward; the days per second set the speed. You can also step a day at a time (hold Shift for ten days), or type a date. Jump to moves to the previous or next opposition, conjunction, greatest elongation or stationary point.",
"Record reading stores the date, the days since your first reading, the planet’s position along the ecliptic (its ecliptic longitude λ), which way it is moving, its angle from the Sun (elongation, positive when the planet is east of the Sun, negative when west), its distance from Earth, how much of it is lit, its size and its phase. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the worksheet; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"See why planets sometimes move backward (west) against the stars, measure how often it happens, and use the phases of Venus to test whether the planets go around Earth or around the Sun.",
"<strong>Retrograde motion.</strong> Night after night, a planet usually drifts slowly east among the stars. For a few weeks at a time, though, it stops, moves west for a while (retrograde motion), stops again and goes on east, tracing a loop or a zigzag. In a Sun centered picture this happens when Earth, on its faster inside track, passes an outer planet, the way a car you overtake on the highway seems to slide backward against the distant hills. Earth passes Mars at <strong>opposition</strong>, when Mars is opposite the Sun in our sky (elongation {#}°), so it rises at sunset and is up all night.",
"<strong>Position on the sky.</strong> The demo gives a planet’s position as its <strong>ecliptic longitude</strong> λ, the angle measured eastward along the ecliptic from the point where the Sun crosses the celestial equator in March. While a planet moves east λ grows; while it is retrograde λ shrinks.",
"<strong>Synodic and sidereal periods.</strong> The <strong>sidereal period</strong> \\(P\\) is the time a planet takes to go once around the Sun. The <strong>synodic period</strong> \\(S\\) is the time from one opposition to the next (or, for Mercury and Venus, from one inferior conjunction, when the planet passes between Earth and the Sun, to the next). Since Earth laps the planet like a runner on a track, the two are related by",
"\\[\\frac{1}{S} = \\left| \\frac{1}{P} - \\frac{1}{365.26\\ \\text{days}} \\right|.\\]",
"<strong>World models.</strong> Ptolemy (about {#} CE) kept Earth at rest. Each planet rides a small circle, the <strong>epicycle</strong>, whose center goes around Earth on a larger circle, the <strong>deferent</strong>. Copernicus ({#}) put the Sun at the center. Tycho Brahe ({#}) kept Earth at rest but had the other planets circle the Sun while the Sun circles Earth. All three can make retrograde loops. In {#} Galileo turned a telescope on Venus and watched its phases.",
"Before the activity",
"Mars goes around the Sun in {#} days. Use the formula above to predict its synodic period. How many years is that?",
"At opposition, is Mars up in the evening, all night, or only before sunrise? Explain with a sketch of the Sun, Earth and Mars.",
"Venus’s orbit is inside Earth’s. Can Venus ever be at opposition? What is the largest angle from the Sun you would expect for a planet whose orbit is {#} times the size of Earth’s? (Hint: draw the line of sight from Earth that just touches Venus’s orbit.)",
"If Venus went around the Sun, when would it look full: when it is on the far side of the Sun or the near side? When would it look biggest?",
"No hazards in the virtual activity.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Mars loops.</strong> Choose Mars loops, {#} to {#}. Record a reading on the opening date, {#} September {#}, then step the date forward {#} days at a time and record a reading at each step, through {#} May {#} ({#} readings). Then jump to each of the two stationary points and to the opposition between them, and record a reading at each (three more readings). Watch the view from above as you go: where is Earth compared with Mars when Mars moves backward? Send the readings to the worksheet before you clear them.",
"<strong>Part {#}. Synodic periods.</strong> Set the date to {#} January {#}. For Mars, Jupiter and Saturn in turn, jump to the next opposition and write down its date, then jump to the one after it and write down that date. For Venus, do the same with the next two inferior conjunctions. Write the sidereal period P from the readouts too. Fill in the second table by hand.",
"<strong>Part {#}. The phases of Venus.</strong> Clear the readings and choose Phases of Venus. Record a reading on the opening date, {#} November {#}, then every {#} days through {#} January {#} ({#} readings), watching the telescope view. Send the readings to the worksheet. Then clear the readings, choose Galileo’s test: Ptolemy’s Venus, record readings on the same {#} dates, and send those to the worksheet too (they go into a second copy of the third table).",
